to-nada-amin-source : List Word
to-nada-amin-source = lines-of (cons "# Nada Amin"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Associate professor of computer science at Harvard SEAS, where she leads the metareflection group. Since July 2026 she has also been an associate faculty member of the Kempner Institute."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is credited to you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work established"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field reads *Collapsing Towers of Interpreters* (with Tiark Rompf, POPL 2018) as a performance result: a stack of interpreters, each running the next, flattened into one compiler. That reading is true, but it is the smaller half of the paper."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The larger half is stage polymorphism. One evaluator, written once, interprets or generates code depending on a binding-time parameter, so interpreting a program and compiling it are one object read at two binding times. Pink shows this holds through any number of levels of self-interpretation, including levels whose semantics were modified. Purple, the reflective language, keeps it holding while a user program changes its own semantics at run time and is recompiled under the change. The infinite tower has been a thought experiment since Smith's 3-Lisp; Purple makes it an executable object that compiles. What follows is a claim about meaning, not speed: a program means the same thing at every height of the tower."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your current line makes the same move with a different machine. In VerMCTS (2024), and in DafnySynth (the 2026 Kempner Accelerator Award project, which writes, proves and repairs software), a language model proposes and a verifier decides. The usual framing treats the model as the intelligence and the verifier as a filter. Yours reverses it: the verifier is what makes the search well-founded, because every point it keeps is correct. You said as much in the Kempner announcement: intelligent systems that are correct by construction."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Specialization with its evidence.** A residual expression satisfies the specialization equation, its meaning on an input equal to the interpreter's"
  (cons (cite s6-p14 "(6.4),")
  (cons "and the first Futamura projection is an instance of that equality; the higher projections are consequences of the defined evaluator and substitution laws, not foundations of the language"
  (cons (cite s6-p15 "(§6).")
  (cons "A staged specializer has an exact type and law"
  (cons (cite s6-1-p5 "(§6.1).")
  (cons "The specializer returns a new code together with a code for its justification: normalization gives a representation definitionally convertible to the input, and theorem-directed rewriting gives an explicit path, so a wrong theorem application cannot be admitted merely because the transformed code is well formed"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2).")
  (cons "Your observation that interpretation and compilation are one object at two binding times is here a typed equation the language holds."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Reflection on the language's own definition.** A source declaration is data, its meaning a term, its transformations terms, their evidence a term, and their results available to further computation"
  (cons (cite s6-1-p26 "(§6.1).")
  (cons "A language's grammar, typing judgments and interpreter are themselves expressions, and a transformation can act on the interpreter, not only on its input programs"
  (cons (cite s6-3-p2 "(§6.3);")
  (cons "transforming its represented clauses changes the interpretation of later inputs"
  (cons (cite s1-p28 "(§1).")
  (cons "When the checker, specializer, compiler and evaluator are themselves represented terms, the native evaluator runs programs that inspect, transform and realize programs, their own presentations included; checker soundness and compiler correctness keep their separate proof obligations"
  (cons (cite s9-2-p14 "(§9.2, closure under reflection).")
  (cons "That is Purple's discipline, with the obligations stated."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The checker inside the language.** The checker is itself a term, and the specializer's results are admitted through the same checking relation as ordinary definitions"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2).")
  (cons "A specialization is accepted by conversion or a checked path, rather than by agreement of sample outputs"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p2 "(§6.2).")
  (cons "Your pairing of a generator with a verifier is the working form of that arrangement in today's tools, with Dafny and Lean on the checking side."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this matters beyond the field's frame"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Read together, your two lines of work make one argument: meaning is invariant under the machinery that executes it, however deep the tower and however much it rewrites itself, and a search for programs is sound when every step is checked. Hyperactivity is built on both."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is yours, in full: stage polymorphism and the collapse of towers (with Tiark Rompf), Pink and Purple, the reflective treatment of user-modified semantics, and the verifier-guided synthesis line from VerMCTS to DafnySynth. Avik wrote this for you so you read it the way you read a colleague's draft, looking for where your ideas went and what more they can do."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Nada Amin, Harvard SEAS, https://seas.harvard.edu/person/nada-amin"
  (cons "- Kempner Institute associate faculty appointment, Harvard Gazette, July 2026, https://news.harvard.edu/gazette/story/newsplus/nada-amin-sitan-chen-appointed-kempner-institute-associate-faculty-members/"
  (cons "- Collapsing Towers of Interpreters, POPL 2018, https://namin.seas.harvard.edu/pubs/popl-collapsing-towers.pdf"
  (cons "- VerMCTS, https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.08147v2"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
